# Docker build context.
#
# This file is a security boundary as much as a speed one. Everything the
# context contains is uploaded to the daemon and is readable by any RUN
# instruction and by anyone who later pulls an intermediate layer — so a stray
# .env or secrets.json in the working tree would end up inside the image even
# though no COPY names it. The secret rules near the bottom are the ones that
# matter; the rest is build hygiene.
#
# Note the interaction with the Dockerfile: it copies only project files first
# to cache `dotnet restore`, then copies the rest. Excluding bin/ and obj/ here
# is what stops a host build's artefacts from leaking in and defeating that
# cache — or worse, being published as the app.

# ── Build output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/out/
**/publish/
**/TestResults/
**/BenchmarkDotNet.Artifacts/
**/*.user
**/*.rsuser
**/*.suo

# ── IDE and tooling ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude/
*.swp

# ── Version control and CI ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .git is the big one: it carries the entire history, which is usually larger
# than the source and has no business inside an image.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/

# ── Docker's own files ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml

# ── Documentation and assets ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Excluded from the image, not from the repo. None of it is read at runtime.
*.md
docs/
LICENSE

# ── SECRETS — never allow these into a layer ───────────────────────────────
# Each is gitignored too, so in a clean checkout none exists. These rules cover
# the working tree of the person running `docker build`, which is where they
# actually do exist.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/secrets.json
**/appsettings.Development.json
**/appsettings.Local.json
**/appsettings.*.Local.json
**/local.settings.json
**/*.pfx
**/*.p12
**/*.pem
**/*.key
